1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,009
Why is my Christmas tree dying?!

2
00:00:02,189 --> 00:00:04,479
Professor Poppleton here! Let's POP the mystery!

3
00:00:04,659 --> 00:00:07,128
Here's the twist. It started dying

4
00:00:07,128 --> 00:00:08,725
the moment it was cut.

5
00:00:09,355 --> 00:00:10,921
And your cosy living room

6
00:00:10,921 --> 00:00:12,048
is speeding it up!

7
00:00:12,528 --> 00:00:15,032
Today's experiment: the thirst, the sealed

8
00:00:15,032 --> 00:00:16,761
cut, and the falling needles.

9
00:00:16,941 --> 00:00:19,569
Three clues, one mystery. Let's crack it.

10
00:00:20,149 --> 00:00:22,278
Guess first! How much water can a

11
00:00:22,278 --> 00:00:24,342
fresh-cut tree drink on day 1? A

12
00:00:24,342 --> 00:00:26,406
cup, half a gallon to 2 gallons,

13
00:00:26,406 --> 00:00:27,374
or none at all?

14
00:00:30,474 --> 00:00:31,734
Lock in your answer. We'll

15
00:00:31,734 --> 00:00:32,849
measure it in a moment.

16
00:00:33,329 --> 00:00:35,400
So, experiment one. Is a

17
00:00:35,400 --> 00:00:37,212
cut tree still alive?

18
00:00:37,392 --> 00:00:39,332
Yes! Its needles are still alive,

19
00:00:39,332 --> 00:00:41,566
breathing and losing water every hour.

20
00:00:41,746 --> 00:00:43,480
Think of it like flowers in a

21
00:00:43,480 --> 00:00:45,990
vase: alive, but living on borrowed water.

22
00:00:46,620 --> 00:00:49,253
Fresh-cut trees have a moisture

23
00:00:49,253 --> 00:00:51,037
content of over 100%.

24
00:00:51,217 --> 00:00:53,233
That means more water than dry

25
00:00:53,233 --> 00:00:55,181
wood, by weight. Your tree is

26
00:00:55,181 --> 00:00:57,532
basically a giant green water tank!

27
00:00:57,712 --> 00:00:59,832
Which, I must say, is more water

28
00:00:59,832 --> 00:01:01,620
than my hair has ever seen.

29
00:01:02,300 --> 00:01:03,271
But a tree with no

30
00:01:03,271 --> 00:01:04,673
roots can't drink. Can it?

31
00:01:04,853 --> 00:01:06,816
Therefore, it drinks the only way

32
00:01:06,816 --> 00:01:08,363
it can: up through the cut

33
00:01:08,363 --> 00:01:09,553
trunk, like a straw.

34
00:01:09,733 --> 00:01:11,344
A 6-ft fir can easily use

35
00:01:11,344 --> 00:01:12,697
4 quarts a day in its

36
00:01:12,697 --> 00:01:14,888
first week. That's a whole gallon!

37
00:01:15,068 --> 00:01:16,947
That's 5 to 7 gallons in the

38
00:01:16,947 --> 00:01:19,027
first week alone, by our maths.

39
00:01:19,207 --> 00:01:20,285
Then about 2 to 3

40
00:01:20,285 --> 00:01:21,554
quarts a day for the

41
00:01:21,554 --> 00:01:22,696
next 3 to 4 weeks.

42
00:01:22,876 --> 00:01:24,868
The experts' rule of thumb: about

43
00:01:24,868 --> 00:01:26,256
a quart a day for every

44
00:01:26,256 --> 00:01:27,584
inch across the trunk.

45
00:01:27,764 --> 00:01:29,724
Now picture all that pouring up through

46
00:01:29,724 --> 00:01:31,181
a stump with no roots at all!

47
00:01:31,961 --> 00:01:33,980
Remember your guess? Here's the answer.

48
00:01:34,610 --> 00:01:35,937
Half a gallon to 2

49
00:01:35,937 --> 00:01:37,707
gallons, on day 1 alone!

50
00:01:37,887 --> 00:01:39,731
Click on subscribe, so your brain

51
00:01:39,731 --> 00:01:40,961
can be as big as mine!

52
00:01:41,641 --> 00:01:43,277
But here's the catch. A tree

53
00:01:43,277 --> 00:01:45,030
heals its wounds, even a wound

54
00:01:45,030 --> 00:01:46,199
you made with a saw.

55
00:01:46,379 --> 00:01:47,865
Out of water for more than

56
00:01:47,865 --> 00:01:49,408
about a quarter of an hour,

57
00:01:49,408 --> 00:01:51,409
the cut starts sealing itself shut.

58
00:01:51,589 --> 00:01:53,052
Hmm. So a tree from the

59
00:01:53,052 --> 00:01:54,834
lot can stand in your water,

60
00:01:54,834 --> 00:01:56,424
sealed, and barely drink.

61
00:01:56,604 --> 00:01:58,652
That's why Penn State's tree experts

62
00:01:58,652 --> 00:02:00,245
recut trees cut more than 12

63
00:02:00,245 --> 00:02:01,952
hours ago: a fresh slice about

64
00:02:01,952 --> 00:02:03,147
a quarter inch thick.

65
00:02:03,327 --> 00:02:04,971
And if the stand runs dry?

66
00:02:04,971 --> 00:02:07,246
Oregon State's expert says most firs

67
00:02:07,246 --> 00:02:09,522
recover if refilled within 24 hours.

68
00:02:09,522 --> 00:02:11,355
Grand fir gets only about 12.

69
00:02:11,535 --> 00:02:12,780
A fresh cut opens those

70
00:02:12,780 --> 00:02:14,132
water tubes again, so the

71
00:02:14,132 --> 00:02:15,594
drinking can start back up.

72
00:02:16,374 --> 00:02:17,923
Pop quiz! What keeps a cut

73
00:02:17,923 --> 00:02:20,247
tree freshest? Sugar water, plain clean

74
00:02:20,247 --> 00:02:21,857
water, or a splash of soda?

75
00:02:25,307 --> 00:02:28,450
Plain water! Extension experts say clean

76
00:02:28,450 --> 00:02:30,493
water is all it needs, and

77
00:02:30,493 --> 00:02:33,636
some additives make needles drop faster.

78
00:02:33,816 --> 00:02:36,524
Sorry, sugar fans. Plain water wins!

79
00:02:37,304 --> 00:02:38,898
Science deep dive! Why do

80
00:02:38,898 --> 00:02:40,811
the needles actually fall off?

81
00:02:40,991 --> 00:02:42,398
Cut off from its roots, a

82
00:02:42,398 --> 00:02:44,199
fir makes a gas called ethylene.

83
00:02:44,379 --> 00:02:45,718
Yes, the same gas plant

84
00:02:45,718 --> 00:02:47,349
hormone that ripens bananas!

85
00:02:47,529 --> 00:02:49,770
Picture every needle buckled to its

86
00:02:49,770 --> 00:02:51,435
twig with a tiny seatbelt.

87
00:02:51,615 --> 00:02:54,438
Ethylene is the signal that says: unbuckle!

88
00:02:55,068 --> 00:02:56,666
In tests on cut balsam

89
00:02:56,666 --> 00:02:59,062
fir branches, untreated ones were

90
00:02:59,062 --> 00:03:01,240
completely bare after 40 days.

91
00:03:01,420 --> 00:03:03,668
Block the ethylene, and needles held

92
00:03:03,668 --> 00:03:04,979
on for up to 87 days!

93
00:03:04,979 --> 00:03:07,540
Steady, Poppleton... no, I'm too excited!

94
00:03:08,520 --> 00:03:10,202
So why is your tree dying

95
00:03:10,202 --> 00:03:11,951
faster than the one at the

96
00:03:11,951 --> 00:03:14,238
lot? Look around your living room.

97
00:03:14,418 --> 00:03:17,893
At 21°C, about 70°F, a cut tree

98
00:03:17,893 --> 00:03:22,040
breathes about 4x faster than at 5°C.

99
00:03:22,220 --> 00:03:25,004
Heaters, vents and sunny windows dry it

100
00:03:25,004 --> 00:03:27,431
out even faster. Penn State says a

101
00:03:27,431 --> 00:03:29,501
cooler room slows the drying.

102
00:03:29,681 --> 00:03:31,062
Poor thing. It's like being

103
00:03:31,062 --> 00:03:32,188
sent to the beach, but

104
00:03:32,188 --> 00:03:33,365
you can't have a drink.

105
00:03:33,545 --> 00:03:35,151
No wonder the tree at the

106
00:03:35,151 --> 00:03:37,272
chilly lot looked so perky, while

107
00:03:37,272 --> 00:03:39,135
yours droops by the radiator.

108
00:03:39,915 --> 00:03:41,472
And a dry tree isn't

109
00:03:41,472 --> 00:03:43,030
just sad. It's fuel.

110
00:03:43,210 --> 00:03:45,840
In fire tests, very dry Douglas-fir

111
00:03:45,840 --> 00:03:48,094
released up to 3,000 kW within

112
00:03:48,094 --> 00:03:50,122
a minute. 500 is enough for

113
00:03:50,122 --> 00:03:52,226
a room to flash over. That's

114
00:03:52,226 --> 00:03:53,879
6x more, by our maths.

115
00:03:54,059 --> 00:03:56,384
US fire departments go to about

116
00:03:56,384 --> 00:03:58,334
155 home fires a year that

117
00:03:58,334 --> 00:04:00,359
start with Christmas trees.

118
00:04:00,539 --> 00:04:02,656
Penn State's simple test: if needles

119
00:04:02,656 --> 00:04:04,068
snap or fall off in your

120
00:04:04,068 --> 00:04:05,774
hand, the tree has dried out.

121
00:04:05,954 --> 00:04:08,654
In tests, Douglas-fir only starts failing

122
00:04:08,654 --> 00:04:10,827
a flammability test once it dries

123
00:04:10,827 --> 00:04:12,934
to about 68% moisture. Down from

124
00:04:12,934 --> 00:04:14,316
over 100% when fresh.

125
00:04:15,096 --> 00:04:17,130
So here's the whole machine. The cut

126
00:04:17,130 --> 00:04:19,106
seals, less water gets in, the warm

127
00:04:19,106 --> 00:04:21,309
room pulls more out, and ethylene tells

128
00:04:21,309 --> 00:04:22,551
the needles to let go.

129
00:04:22,731 --> 00:04:24,600
Every turn of the loop, the tree

130
00:04:24,600 --> 00:04:26,819
gets drier. Keep water flowing in, and

131
00:04:26,819 --> 00:04:28,337
the loop slows right down.

132
00:04:28,517 --> 00:04:31,789
Mississippi State's forestry expert says a fresh

133
00:04:31,789 --> 00:04:33,971
tree, well cared for, lasts 3 to

134
00:04:33,971 --> 00:04:35,471
4 weeks, often even 5.

135
00:04:36,251 --> 00:04:38,119
THE BIG POP: your tree is dying

136
00:04:38,119 --> 00:04:40,227
of thirst. Its cut seals shut, your

137
00:04:40,227 --> 00:04:42,516
warm room dries it, and ethylene tells

138
00:04:42,516 --> 00:04:44,384
the needles to let go. 1 gallon

139
00:04:44,384 --> 00:04:45,649
a day keeps it going.

140
00:04:45,829 --> 00:04:47,571
So it isn't a curse. It's

141
00:04:47,571 --> 00:04:49,453
thirst, a sealed cut, and a

142
00:04:49,453 --> 00:04:50,916
gas that says let go.

143
00:04:52,096 --> 00:04:54,075
How many weeks does your tree usually

144
00:04:54,075 --> 00:04:55,680
last? Tell me in the comments.

145
00:04:55,860 --> 00:04:58,213
Fresh-cut trees are more than half water,

146
00:04:58,213 --> 00:04:59,992
and now you know where it goes.

147
00:05:00,172 --> 00:05:01,758
Mine would last about a day. I'd

148
00:05:01,758 --> 00:05:04,037
forget the water while reading about ethylene!

149
00:05:04,217 --> 00:05:06,346
A new science mystery every week.

150
00:05:06,526 --> 00:05:08,890
Stay curious, and keep asking why!

